  Parity reproduced from the web source (every REAL branch — no fabricated chrome):
   - labelled radiogroup of 5 paint swatches (web PAINT_PALETTE_LIST.map): opaque swatch dot (web
     p.swatch), selected ring + white checkmark (web `border-white scale-110` + check SVG), the
     Auto-detected hint on the inferred swatch (web `title = label · Auto-detected`)
   - the live current-paint name (web aria-live span `t(paint.labelKey, paint.defaultLabel)`)
   - the Reset affordance shown only while a local override is active (web `{isOverridden && <button>}`)
   - setPaint(inferred.id) normalized to "clear the override" (web `id === inferred.id ? null : id`) so
     the picker re-syncs if Tesla later reports a colour
   - Tesla `exterior_color` inference across every variant + the Pearl-White fallback (web
     inferPaintFromTesla / FALLBACK_PAINT)
   - once-only view.opened (P1/S11)
   - native "never a blank box" empty leaf for a degenerate catalog (no palettes)
  Faithful-parity note: the web <VehiclePaintPicker> is a PURELY presentational control (hooks:
  useTranslation / useVehiclePaint; NO fetcher — useVehiclePaint is a synchronous, browser-local
  localStorage override store, not a React-Query feed). It therefore has NO loading / error / stale /
  offline async branch — there is nothing to fetch, fail, age, or lose connectivity to. This surface
  reproduces the source's real branches only, the same precedent as siblings ThemePicker (0228) &
  ThemeProvider (0229). Inventing async chrome would fabricate states the source does not have (Honesty
  Covenant #3 No skip-and-assume / #5 No parity shortcuts cut both ways).

